{"chunk_id":"R4-2601172-002","collection":"ran4_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Samsung"],"tdocNumber":"R4-2601172","title":"Discussion on RAN4 operation efficiency for CR handling"},"point_id":"14103777310315508151"}
 experience, the specification editors’ role is demonstrated to be critical in controlling specification quality—serving as both technical gatekeepers and cross-domain coordinators who reconcile conflicting requirements, standardize terminology, and ensure end-to-end consistency across documents. For 6G, RAN4 specifications face even greater complexity than their 5G predecessors: they span an unprecedentedly broad scope, from more advanced RF technologies to next-generation baseband processing. This expanded remit—coupled with the need to integrate various new emerging use cases like AI/ML-enabled ones—further underscores why an editor-led procedure is not just important, but indispensable for 6G specification quality control.​ Proposal 1: RAN4 should adopt the structured, specification editor-led procedures for 6G specification quality improvement. From our understanding, at least the following aspects can be improved in which specification editor could/should play a more important role. Running CR approach prior to agreement: To address the growing depth of technical content in 6G RAN4 specs, it is strongly encouraged to adopt a running CR approach that extends the editing window beyond traditional meeting cycles. Under this framework, companies submitting CRs would share drafts to the reflector at least two weeks before the next RAN4 meeting’s Tdoc submission deadline. The editor would then conduct a preliminary review to flag inconsistencies with existing specifications, gaps in technical justification, or other non-compliance issues. This early feedback loop allows submitters to refine their CRs proactively, reducing the volume of last-minute revisions and ensuring that only technically sound proposals are tabled for group discussion. Additionally, the editor would maintain a live, shared repository of pending CRs, enabling real-time collaboration between submitters and editors, and providing visibility to the broader RAN4 working group to prevent duplicate submissions or conflicting proposals.​ Mandatory specification editor review for CR agreement: To strengthen quality control, all CRs drafted by companies must undergo a formal review by the specification editor before being eligible for approval by the RAN4 working group. This review process would be structured around two key pillars: (1) Technical Validity: Verifying that the CR’s proposed changes are technically correctly captured, aligned with existing agreements, and compatible with other specifications; (2) Specification Consistency: Ensuring that the CR does not introduce contradictions with existing clauses, maintains uniform terminology, and adheres to the standardized formatting and structure of RAN4 documents. The editor would provide a CR revision before the CR can be officially agreed.​ Other new approaches (e.g., editor-organized online drafting sessions between RAN4 meetings, collaborative editing tool, etc.): To maintain momentum and address complex technical topics that require iterative refinement, the specification editor could consider to organize regular online drafting sessions during the gap periods between in-person RAN4 meetings. Furthermore RAN4 should also be open to new collaborative editing tool. Proposal 2: For the structured, specification editor-led procedures for 6G specification quality improvement, RAN4 can discuss to consider the following ones: Running CR approach prior to agreement. Mandatory specification editor review for CR agreement. Other new approaches (e.g., editor-organized online drafting sessions between RAN4 meetings, collaborative editing tool, etc.).

